      <h3>5.2 Overseas</h3>
      <p>A product that needs installation cannot be spread across countries at once. After the installation and repair procedures are refined at home, Japan comes first, where equipment standards and gym layouts resemble Korea's, then the United States, the largest market. The US has deep demand from college athletics and performance centres that pay for analysis, but it needs new certification, so it comes later. Europe is last. The Korean filing comes first, then the PCT route within a year into Japan and the US, so that the rights are in place before the market entry.</p>

      <h2 id="status"><span class="num">6</span>Status and open problems</h2>
      <h3>6.1 Built so far</h3>
      <p>Three cameras tied together by a hardware signal capture the same instant. The positions are calibrated, 3D joints are reconstructed, and sets are cut and logged automatically. All of it is built and running in-house. Camera positions were chosen by computing and comparing 34,560 placements, then measured on a PVC rack mock-up. There is a kiosk app that walks through installation, calibration and measurement, TensorRT inference running on the NVIDIA Jetson, and the service structure that returns coaching feedback when a set ends.</p>
      <p>Figure 5 shows the simulation that compared the 34,560 placements and the PVC mock-up with three cameras mounted for the measurement. Figure 6 shows the kiosk guiding lens-distortion and camera-position calibration. The result is checked on screen before moving on to measurement.</p>
      <figure>
        <img src="/assets/plan/coverage.webp" width="1024" height="535" alt="A 3D coverage simulation of camera frustums around a wireframe rack, and a photo of the PVC power-rack mock-up with three cameras mounted" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>Figure 5. Camera-placement sweep and the PVC rack mock-up.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="/assets/plan/calibration.webp" width="1600" height="1057" alt="The kiosk calibration report with a 3D plot of the three camera poses and the error figures, beside photos of the PVC rig with the calibration board and the kiosk's camera view" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>Figure 6. Kiosk during calibration.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>6.2 Open technical problems</h3>
      <p>What could be built in-house has been built. The bottleneck now is not a lack of technology but a lack of grounds for deciding what to build next. Three problems remain.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Occlusion, a fork in the road.</strong> Whether to estimate joints with no observation from a body model, or to add a fourth camera and measure them. The first is cheap but unverified. The second is verified but raises the cost. The choice decides product tier and price at once, and it will be settled by a real placement-sweep experiment and motion-capture studio validation.</li>
        <li><strong>Grounds for the target spec.</strong> We have not yet computed how a 3 cm joint error propagates to the distance between the center of mass and a joint, and on to the estimated joint load. That number is what decides how many centimetres are good enough to ship. Today the judgment constants are hand-set in code and marked as not a calibrated biomechanical model.</li>
        <li><strong>Independent validation.</strong> The accuracy so far was measured by the founder, on a public dataset only. One run gave an encouraging 2.88 cm joint error, until it turned out to count only the well-tracked joints, and it was discarded. A figure against marker-based motion capture is the first gate to commercialisation.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>6.3 Prototype tasks</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Component tiers.</strong> Compute will split into Orin Nano and Orin NX. Which functions the lower tier must carry for cost and performance to balance is undecided.</li>
        <li><strong>Mounting.</strong> The mounts and housings that attach straight to a power rack are due for a 3D-printed redesign that satisfies camera angle, stiffness and vibration together.</li>
        <li><strong>Where hardware meets software.</strong> Only after the third camera went in did it become clear that USB bandwidth runs out and frames get dropped. Ceiling rails will hit the same wall, so the order changes: compute the limits first, then decide the structure, then build.</li>
      </ul>

